ic.  Run it from /dd/DOC/unibasic  COMPILING WITH UNIBASIC, start to finish.  It works; here is what it  wants.  UniBasic translates your BASIC into C, then drives the C  compiler to build a module, so it needs your OS-9 C toolchain:    THESE FOUR STEPS ARE IN THE OS-9 SHELL'S OWN LANGUAGE -- `setenv'    and `chd' are its commands, not bash's.  In bash use `export    NAME=value', and note that bash's `cd' does not move the OS-9    data directory at all (DOC/README-RUNNING says why).    1. SET SHELL.  UniBasic runs its compile through $SHELL.  With SHELL       unset it looks for `/dd/bash' and stops with `Error Exit' and       error 216, so set it:       setenv SHELL /dd/CMDS/sh    2. WORK WHERE basic.h AND basic.l ARE.  chd to a directory holding       both, or copy them beside your program.  DOC/unibasic has them.    3. POINT AT YOUR C TOOLCHAIN.  cc, c68, o68, r68, l68, cpp and merge       on the execution path; setenv CDEF to your headers and CLIB to       your libraries.  The link wants cstart.r, clibn.l and sys.l.    4. GIVE IT ROOM.  The preprocessor says `No more memory !!!' if it       is short; os9exec users want something like -M 128M -mm 8M.  A five-line program written from scratch, compiled by ub68kdemo, ran  and printed its output.  The compiled  bench1..bench11 programs ship beside their sources, so there is  something to run before you set any of this up.  bio, blackjack and wysetime are BASIC09 I-code, and want runb.------------------------------------------------------/dd/CMDS/MM1 -- device drivers for the MM/1 (7)------------------------------------------------------  Drivers and descriptors for the MM/1, one of the machines OS-9/68000  ran on.  These are modules you LOAD, not programs you run; start  one from a shell and it reports that it could not begin, as a driver  should.  Kept because an MM/1 is a real OS-9 machine and somebody still has  one.  On anything else they have no hardware to drive.  keydrv