o *expire and rnews.  This is a complete store-and-forward system: it  dials another machine over a serial line, exchanges mail and news  batches, and runs the jobs the other end queued for you.  Before the  internet reached most people, this WAS how mail arrived.  Kept in its own directory because these eighteen only make sense  together, and because they need a configuration of their own --  those same files are INSTALLED at /dd/SYS/UUCP, which is where the  programs look, so `uuname` already answers with the sample sites  rather than an error.  /dd/DOC/uucpbb/SAMPLE keeps the pristine  copies to go back to.  The manuals are in  /dd/DOC/uucpbb, already formatted; `list` them.  Every one is starred: they were built `cc -qixm` like most of this  collection's era, so they use cio, which ships in /dd/CMDS.  All of them  want a serial port to dial out of, which is what they are here for.  The whole package is GPL v2; /dd/DOC/uucpbb/copying is the licence  as it included. *adduser        add a user to the system, for uucp logins *dotilde        the mailer's TILDE-ESCAPE handler: it                 reads a message from standard input and acts on the `~'                 commands in it, answering `Unrecognized tilde command' and                 `Continuing...type "." or <ESC> to end message...' *expire         delete news articles past their expiry date *fileserv       serve files to remote sites on request *fixtext        repair the line endings of a received text batch *mailx          the mail reader and sender *postnews       post an article to a newsgroup *readnews       read Usenet news articles: it opens the reader and asks                 about each newsgroup in the active file not yet in                 .newsrc, then answers `**** End of newsgroups' when the                 news spool is empty *rmail          deliver incoming mail (invoked by uuxqt, not by you).                 Given a local name it builds <mailbox>/<user>, and the                 mailbox here is a file, so it stops with `can't change                 to mailbox: /dd/SPOOL/MAIL/tester/tester'.  Given a                 `host!user' address for remote delivery it does not                 return at all *rnews          unpack an incoming news batch *subscribe      add a newsgroup to your subscription list -- for one                 already in /dd/.newsrc but turned off, `Newsgroup X is                 now subscribed.' and `X! 1' becomes `X: 1' in the file.                 A group not in .newsrc at all is silently left alone,                 which both this and unsubscribe do *unsubscribe    drop one, and it does that too: `!' replaces `:'.  It has                 one bug of its own -- for a group that is ALREADY off it                 prints `Newsgroup  684700s already unsubscribed.', because                 the string in the binary is `Newsgroup % is already                 unsubscribed.' with no conversion letter after the `%' *uucico         the transfer program itself -- dials, talks UUCP *uuclean        remove stale jobs from the spool.  The spool is                 /dd/SPOOL/uucp, and SYS/UUCP/Parameters names it.  uuclean                 walks every ENTRY in the spool as if it were a directory,                 so the README that keeps the directory in the repository                 trips it: `can't change to directory .../README'.                 Harmless, and it is why the message is not a sign of a                 broken spool *uucp           queue a file copy to or from another site *uulog          show the transfer log *uuname         list the sites you can reach *uuxqt          run the jobs a remote site queued here.  It looks for a                 module called `procs' to see whether it is already                 running, so it wants a `procs' loaded (error 221 without                 one). *disable       disable a UUCP device *enable        re-enable a UUCP device *lcasep        lowe