t back naming every font in it.  The  DVI drivers use the bitmaps: each one runs,  reads the .dvi, writes its output file and says, once per font, `Font  file [cmr10 [300 dpi]] could not be opened ... Proceeding with zero size  characters'.  Making those bitmaps is what MetaFont and MakeTeXPK are  for, and the MetaFont sources to make them from are here.  RUN THE ENGINE.  `tex' and `latex' are one-line wrappers that call  `virtex "&plain"' or `virtex "&lplain"' through a shell; the quoted  format name is not unquoted, so the shell is handed one long path name  and answers E$PNNF.  Call the engine directly:      virtex '&plain'  yourfile.tex        plain TeX      virtex '&lplain' yourfile.tex        LaTeX      virtex '&splain' yourfile.tex        SliTeX  `virtex "&lplain" SYS/TEX/SAMPLES/small.tex' answers `Output written  on small.dvi (1 page, 1704 bytes)', and all ten DVI drivers then read  it.  The .dvi and .log land in the DATA directory.  `dvips' wants a header file tex.pro -- point it at one, or use one of  the ten dvi* drivers; `dvialw' writes PostScript too.  Read /dd/DOC/tex/Installation.text and Usage.text first -- they are the  port's own, and they explain building a format and making fonts.  There  are worked documents in SYS/TEX/SAMPLES, and About_Latex.tex is a LaTeX  article describing this very system.  bibtex         BibTeX -- bibliography formatter, and it WORKS.  It reads                 the job name from STANDARD INPUT (`Please type input file                 name (no extension)--' is a prompt, not silence), and the                 .bst styles are in SYS/TEX/INPUTS -- plain, unsrt, abbrv                 and alpha -- not in SYS/TEX/BIB, which holds a read.me  dvialw         DVI to Apple LaserWriter  dvidjp         DVI to HP DeskJet Plus  dvieps         DVI to Epson  dviimp         DVI to Imagen  dvijep         DVI to HP LaserJet Plus  dvijet         DVI to HP LaserJet  dvilj2         DVI to HP LaserJet II  dv