2 -- cccp2 twice on purpose, because gcc2 forks it as cccp2and gpp forks it as cccp.  The originals stay where they are.  GCC139 isNOT installed this way; its gcc_cc1plus and GCC2's would want one name.THE PROGRAMS:  gcc           the GCC driver -- GCC139's and GCC2's share this name.                They are NOT the same version, and neither is 2.x:                GCC139/gcc answers `gcc version 1.39' and GCC2/gcc                answers `gcc version 1.42', read out of `gcc -v'  gcc2          the GCC 2.x driver, and the only one that is:                `gcc version 2.5.6'  gpp           the C++ driver -- and it is a GCC 1.x one.  GCC2/gpp                says `gpp version 1.40.3 (based on GCC 1.40)' and                GCC139/gpp says 1.37.1  gcc_cc1       GCC 1.39 C compiler pass  gcc_cc1plus   GCC 1.39 C++ compiler pass  gcc_cccp      GCC 1.39 preprocessor  gcc_cccp2     GCC 2.x preprocessor, under the name gcc2 forks  gcc_cc2       GCC 2.x compiler pass, under the name gcc2 forks  gcc_collect   GCC 1.39 collect2  gpp_cccp      GCC 2.x preprocessor, under the name gpp forks  gpp_cc1plus   GCC 2.x C++ pass, under the name gpp forks  gpp_collect   GCC 2.x collect2, under the name gpp forks  cc1plus       GCC 2.x C++ compiler pass, where it was built  cc2           GCC 2.x C compiler pass, where it was built  cc2plus       GCC 2.x C++ pass, second form  cccp2         GCC 2.x preprocessor, where it was built  collect       GCC 2.x collect2, where it was builtgcc compiles to assembly and then needs r68, l68, clibn.l and cstart.r,Microware's.  With your SDK, both compilers work -- C and C++, compiled,linked and run.WHERE A COMPILE REACHES, on a four-line hello.c:  cccp2 file.c file.i        preprocesses.  Works.  gcc2 -S -o file.a file.c   compiles to 68k assembly.  Works, and the                             assembly is Microware's syntax -- `psect',                             `link.w a5,#0', `bsr _stkcheck'.  gcc2 -o file file.c        stops: `Can't fork \'r68\': 000:221 module                             not found'.So everything up to the assembler is here and works; the assembler andlinker are Microware's.  GCC139/gcc forks a module named `os9exec' andfails earlier -- use gcc2.  GCC139 (6) gcc gcc_cc1 gcc_cc1plus gcc_cccp gcc_collect gpp             Use CMDS/makeinfo (GCC139/makeinfo was the same file).  GCC2   (8) cc1plus cc2 cc2plus cccp2 collect gcc gcc2 gpp------------------------------------------------------/dd/CMDS/REBUILT -- alternates kept beside the originals------------------------------------------------------  Alternates for names this disk already uses, other editions of the  same program, and builds for other processors.  The curated binary  is still the one in /dd/CMDS; these are here so you can compare or  swap them yourself.  THE gzip SET IS FOR OTHER CPUs.  CMDS/gzip is 1.2.2 for the 68000.  These are 1.2.4 built six ways -- 68000, 68020 and CPU32, each with  and without csl.  If your machine is a 68020 or a CPU32, one of  these is built for it and CMDS/gzip is not.  compress_4.0   compress 4.0, another edition of CMDS/compress  zoo_2.1        zoo 2.1 (1991), newer than the 2.01 in CMDS.  Same OSK                 porters as pd-ksh.  Reads what 2.01 writes; CMDS/zoo is                 left in place because the pool tools here call it. *gzip68k_csl    gzip 1.2.4, 68000, needs csl  gzip68k_nocsl  gzip 1.2.4, 68000, no csl needed *gzip020_csl    gzip 1.2.4, 68020, needs csl  gzip020_nocsl  gzip 1.2.4, 68020, no csl needed *gzipcpu32k_csl  gzip 1.2.4, CPU32, needs csl  gzipcpu32_nocsl  gzip 1.2.4, CPU32, no csl needed  ctags.elvis    elvis 1.7's ctags; CMDS/ctags is the BSD one  elvis_input    elvis under its `input' personality -- it opens already in                 insert mode.  The name is load-bearing: elvis's wrapper                 picks its personality from the LAST LETTER of the name it                 was invoked by, so a name en